TSM client.dll patch -- main menu ambient music
==================================================

What this is: a binary patch to Half-Life's TS client DLL (cl_dlls\client.dll) that plays a
looping mp3 track while the main menu is up, before the player joins a server. This file
documents exactly what bytes changed and why, byte-for-byte, generated directly from a diff of
the original and patched files rather than written by hand -- so it cannot drift from what the
patch actually does.

Original file : client.dll
Patched file  : client_patched_v4.dll
Original MD5  : cfc60e4e96c51903e6ef7d2b9839b3b7
Patched MD5   : d3210fa4f1b44658f6ea6d5603c87fc8
Original size : 861184 bytes
Patched size  : 865280 bytes (+4096)

Nothing in the original file's bytes is deleted or moved. The patch is: one 5-byte instruction
changed in place, a handful of PE header fields updated to describe a new section, and one new
4096-byte section appended to the end of the file. Every existing function, byte offset, and
relocation entry outside the one 5-byte hook is untouched.


1. The hook -- HUD_VidInit, RVA 0x501f (file offset 0x441f)
-------------------------------------------------------------

HUD_VidInit is a function the engine calls once video mode is set up, early in startup, before
the main menu is shown. Its original code (unchanged apart from the one line below):

    0x5010  mov ecx, 0x100c9d28
    0x5015  call 0x10009d10
    0x501a  call 0x1003db50
    0x501f  mov eax, 1          <-- replaced
    0x5024  ret

The instruction at 0x501f is a 5-byte "mov eax, 1" (bytes: B8 01 00 00 00), which just sets
HUD_VidInit's return value. The patch replaces exactly those 5 bytes with a 5-byte call into new
code appended at the end of the file (bytes: E8 45 30 2D 00 -> call 0x102d8069). Nothing else in
HUD_VidInit moves: the "ret" at 0x5024 is still the original, untouched byte. The new code ends
with its own "mov eax, 1; ret", so it has the same observable effect on the caller as the
instruction it replaced, plus the side effect described below, and control returns to exactly
0x5024 afterward, same as before the patch.

    file offset 0x441f-0x4423:
      original: b8 01 00 00 00    (mov eax, 1)
      patched:  e8 45 30 2d 00    (call 0x102d8069)


2. PE header changes -- registering the new section
-------------------------------------------------------------

  * File header, NumberOfSections (file offset 0x7e): 7 -> 8
  * Optional header, SizeOfImage (file offset 0xc8): 0x2d8000 -> 0x2d9000
  * New section table entry appended (file offset 0x288-0x2b0, the 41-byte-aligned slack that
    already existed after the 7 existing entries -- no existing entry moved):

      Name             .mus
      VirtualAddress   0x2d8000
      VirtualSize      0x1000
      PointerToRawData 0xd2400  (immediately after the original file's last byte)
      SizeOfRawData    0x1000
      Characteristics  0x60000020  (CODE | EXECUTE | READ -- not writable)

These three edits are the standard, minimal way to add a new section to a PE image; nothing
about any existing section (.text, .rdata, .data, .reloc, etc.) is touched.


3. The new .mus section (file offset 0xd2400, 4096 bytes, all new)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Laid out as:

  0x2d8000  UTF-16 path, "ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt" (54 bytes, null-terminated)
  0x2d8034  ASCII fallback track name, "vangelis.mp3" (13 bytes, null-terminated)
  0x2d8041  ASCII command template, "MP3Volume 1\nmp3 on\nmp3 loop sound/music/" (40 bytes)
  0x2d8069  the actual code (160 bytes); the rest of the section is unused zero padding

What the code does, once, the first time HUD_VidInit runs (guarded by a flag so it cannot
re-trigger on a later video mode change):

  1. Opens "ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt" (relative to the game's working directory, i.e. right next
     to this DLL) via CreateFileW/ReadFile/CloseHandle -- all three already imported by the
     original client.dll from KERNEL32.dll, so no new imports were added either.
  2. If the file is missing, reads its contents (up to 4095 bytes) into a scratch buffer.
  3. Splits the contents into lines. A line is ignored if it is empty or its first character is
     '#'. Every other line is recorded as a candidate track filename.
  4. If zero candidates were found (file missing, empty, or all comments), the fallback name
     "vangelis.mp3" is used. If exactly one candidate was found, that one is used. If more than
     one, one is chosen uniformly at random via the engine's own pfnRandomLong function (the same
     engine call GoldSrc game code normally uses for randomness -- no separate RNG was written).
  5. Builds the final string "MP3Volume 1\nmp3 on\nmp3 loop sound/music/<chosen name>\n" and sends
     it exactly once via the engine's pfnClientCmd, the same client-command mechanism the TS
     server plugin (ts_matchmode.sma) already uses for its own in-map ambient jukebox.

All scratch buffers the code writes to at runtime (the read buffer, the candidate list, the final
command string, the one-shot guard flag) live in client.dll's own .data section, in its existing
zero-filled slack space -- no new writable memory was added either; .mus itself is marked
read+execute only, consistent with it containing code and constants, never anything written at
runtime.

Every address the new code touches -- the engine function table, the KERNEL32 imports, its own
constants, its own scratch variables -- is computed at runtime relative to the module's own
actual load address (via a standard position-independent "call/pop" sequence at the top of the
stub), rather than being a fixed address baked into the file. This means the new code needs no
new entries in the .reloc directory and works correctly regardless of where Windows actually
loads the DLL -- confirmed by checking that no relocation entry exists inside either the hook
bytes or the new section (an earlier draft of this same feature corrupted itself exactly this way
by overwriting bytes that still had a stale relocation entry registered against them; this
version was built specifically to avoid that class of bug).


4. The config file
-------------------------------------------------------------

Location: ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt, next to client.dll itself.

Format: one mp3 filename per line (matching a file already in sound\music\). Blank lines and
lines starting with '#' are ignored. If the file is missing, or has no usable lines, the DLL
falls back to "vangelis.mp3". If it lists more than one track, one is picked at random every time
the game starts.

Changing the menu music later never requires touching the DLL: edit this text file.


5. Rollback
-------------------------------------------------------------

The original, unmodified client.dll (MD5 cfc60e4e96c51903e6ef7d2b9839b3b7) is kept alongside this
patch. Restoring it is a single file copy back over ts\cl_dlls\client.dll, with the game closed.
